just last year fossils of our species  were dated to 300,000 years ago much  earlier than anyone had suspected but  archeologists have struggled to find  many securely dated fossils and stone  tools from this critical time now a team  of researchers have uncovered a panoply  of evidence of early human behavior  dating back three hundred and twenty  thousand years ago to the middle Stone  Age from the use of sophisticated tools  to social networks and pigments this  toolkit emerged shortly after our  ancestors brains underwent a big surge  in size four hundred thousand years ago  and just before fossils that may be very  early members of our species appeared  the research is part of a multi-decade  project at sites on the allure gastly  basin of southern Kenya there the team  excavated some small carefully shaped  tools made of obsidian which is valued  for its sharpness the tools were made by  striking small blades and points often a  symmetrical core into a desired shape  and thickness this suggests complex  planning more than the earlier  relatively clunky large stone tools such  as the hand axe or scraper the opposite  ends of the points were sometimes  modified to allow them to be attached to  shafts this projectile technology is an  important step in the development of  hunting for fast and dangerous prey  these tools could be flung at a distance  with reduced danger to the hunter the  team analyzed the stone artifacts and  compared them to locations of obsidian  sources in Kenya they found that the  rocks must have been transported from as  far as 90 kilometres away farther than  individuals were likely to walk this  suggests that the rock for making stone  tools and other objects may have been  passed hand to hand in a network of  ancient humans such networks would have  required considerable social and  cognitive complexity this is the  earliest evidence of human social  networks to date just as remarkable was  the discovery of rocks used for  colouring one site had rocks streaked  with black manganese and other sites  revealed process  red ocher these pigments may have been  used to draw on people's bodies as a  form of symbolic identity if you have a  red streak on your forehead it signals  to others possibly hostile strangers  that you're the person they met with  before and that you're trustworthy so  why and how did these shifts and  behavior happen with middle Stone Age  hominids researchers examined a plethora  of evidence from the base and sediment  and found at the time in which these  individuals emerged was one of severe  resource and stability and episodes of  scarcity these ancient humans may have  expanded their social networks and  hunting skills as a means to survive  such unpredictable changing conditions  researchers have yet to uncover hominin  fossils from the Middle Stone Age at a  lore gastly but the tools tell us much  about their makers these new discoveries  provide strong support that some aspects  of modern behavior have deep roots and  emerged early in Africa then evolved  gradually over time  [Music]  you  you
 

